This program REPLACES the runtime's write/exit provider (#1367 SEAM 3), so it
never reaches `statsReport` and emits no `[bit-gc] collections=...` line however
`BIT_GC_STATS` is set. Its own `exit_group` syscall is the thing under test: the
harness's aggregate-line requirement is a property of the standard boot path, and
this program deliberately is not on it.

Declared rather than tolerated, and the distinction matters. A missing stats line
is normally FATAL, because it is what a broken environment or a broken capture
looks like — and treating it as "this build does not report the field" would turn
the collection oracle off for the whole corpus in one line with no error text
anywhere. This file says the absence is a property of THIS program, and the cost
is stated: the collection and abandonment oracles do not apply here. The stdout
comparison against `rootiolinux.expected` still does, under both policies.

WRITTEN FROM A MEASUREMENT, NOT FROM INFERENCE, and that is why it did not land
with its twin. `../rootiodarwin/no-stats` predicted this file in its own last
paragraph and deliberately declined to write it, on the grounds that a marker
justified by symmetry alone outlives its reason. The prediction is now confirmed:
`scripts/arm64gate.sh` on aarch64-linux reports

    stress 'rootiolinux' [default]: no '[bit-gc] collections=' line
    stress: FAIL - 59 programs run, 19 skipped, 1 failed

with `BIT_GC_STATS=1` set for that run, on a host that actually runs the case.
That was the remaining half of #1875 — the other half was a genuine bug in the
Linux netpoller (#1910) and is fixed in code, not waived here.
